{
  "dom_id": "step-q115935",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/57ecd122ba41b296b3884ecd4f748f6b4ac4bd1d26f14d7b5166c5fe6e33674b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(1-y)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y-1)^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/e109d237419aaba5efcc0c65c2d97c8f8ded17d18f98bb0a95eba3336d224590.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)(1-y)$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y-1)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
